abstract: >-
  Reproducible framework for sharp finite-target partial identification of a
  fixed quantum model's predictive advantage over the best member of a
  prespecified fixed classical-kernel reference family under distribution shift. The v1.1 theory
  covers any additive bounded loss on a finite label space and proves
  sharpness and minimality; zero-one accuracy admits exact closed-form
  zero-label endpoints and pathwise-exact partial-label updates. The framework combines SVC and
  Laplace Gaussian-process quantum-kernel comparisons on EMBER, UNSW-NB15,
  ToN-IoT, and TableShift with a 30/60/115 reference-breadth frontier,
  physically separated prediction-label locks, acquisition controls,
  circuit-aware entangling/product strata, and repeated finite-shot
  perturbations. Prospective corroboration on two technically eligible tasks
  met the predefined Gate-2 criteria against the prespecified classical-kernel
  reference family; a third
  task failed its predeclared feature gate before model execution.

